hi everyone I'm Hannah Davis and today  I'm going to be talking to you about  generating music from text and emotion  and other experiments and when I give  this talk I use usually like to talk a  little bit about data sonification first  and data sonification is exactly like  data visualization except instead of  representing data through visuals you're  representing it through sound I'll show  you a few examples of what I mean  [Music]  that's one of my favorites so this next  one is very different it's kind of  basically it's sauna fiying how fast  Olympic athletes cross the finish line  and I love this piece this is by Amanda  Cox who's kind of a legend I love this  piece because if you see this image you  can you can see how fast they are and  you know how close they are together but  when you listen to it the impact is much  stronger this next one is by James  Murphy of LCD soundsystem and this is  different than the other two in that it  takes kind of a lot of components of the  data this is data from tennis matches  and it takes a bunch of these components  like the player names the seeds the  temperature which court they were on and  combines all of those to make kind of a  more atmospheric piece  [Music]  so there are a couple different types of  data sonification the first is ah de  fication and when people get into this  field this is kind of where they start  this is just directly converting data to  sound interpreting data as amplitude  over time but what I'm more interested  in and what I'll be talking about a  little more today are these next two  which are parameter mapping which is  where you're mapping some value of the  data to the component of the sound with  the focus being on highlighting the data  and then music generation which is the  same thing your mapping values of data  to values of the sound but the focus is  not on conveying the data as much as it  is about creating interesting music and  sound has a couple of strengths that  visuals don't necessarily have its  temporal its multi-dimensional it's  really easy to find cycles and patterns  and just finding structure in data it's  good for small units it's good for  streams of data and hearing changes in  streams like the first stock exchange  when we saw it's good for grabbing  attention and humor and finally it's  it's naturally emotional and it moves  the listener so before I continue with  music I want to just talk about my own  background in emotional data I first got  interested in emotional data when I  found this data set maybe five or six  years ago called singularly how did the  candidates make you feel and all this  was was just asking voters how the  presidential candidates made them feel  and when I was prototyping like a data  visualization no matter what I did I  couldn't convey what that question was  really entailing and around the same  time I found out about these Chernov  phases which in data visualization I  think are a little bit look down upon  because it's hard to distinguish the  different parts of data you know you're  mapping different parts of the data to  different features of the face but you  can see this one here is like a not good  one these are these are like applicant  scores so the eyebrows are related to  the autobiography whatever that means  the smile size is related to the  interviews  core and the visualization here is just  not really relevant to the underlying  data but I thought it would be relevant  to the how did the candidates make you  feel and so I ended up mapping this  question to these chair and off phases  and so basically the anger is related to  the slope of the eyebrows the fear was  related to the width the size of the  eyes the pride was related to the length  of the nose and the smile was related to  the width of the mouth and so what that  ended up doing was I think over time  really representing how people felt  about these elections like I was at  Harvard that one but this one was pretty  meh for like everyone you know no one  was really feeling it either way this  one we all remember like very a lot of  extremes woodbush and then of course we  had the Obama Hope time period and then  unfortunately the datasets stopped there  and this is like something they'd been  tracking for ever but I would have loved  to see in this last election or maybe I  wouldn't have we'll see so I was  interested in emotional data for a while  this was me just like tracking my own  facial expressions while browsing the  internet where each second was each each  square was a second and I used this kind  of visualization to do my first  sonification and this was representing  different authors writing styles  [Music]  and I liked this piece a lot because you  really can hear the different writing  styles come through like Hemingway has  no descriptive words at all  [Music]  so I did that project for a while but  ultimately felt like the pieces that  were coming out were too similar and so  I started thinking about like what is  interesting to people about you know  their favorite novels and things like  that and it's the emotions and so I  wanted to make a project where I could  do the same thing but with the emotions  and so I had two questions for myself in  starting out this project and the first  was can I translate emotions between  mediums can I make a piece of music that  has the emotions of a novel and can I  create a musical piece with the same  underlying tone as the novel all  programmatically and so for a few years  I worked on a project called trans trans  prose which is basically a mapping  between text analysis and music  composition and how it works is I'm  using a word emotion association lexicon  to get these splines of emotion counts  throughout the novel and increase I  increase the melodic movement at  particularly emotional points and what  this means is that the plot is created  not in the literal events but in the  emotional representation of the events  and I'll play you a few pieces here but  before I do I want to play you a few  things that went wrong so I can  appropriately set your expectations the  first couple of pieces were missing a  few things including complexity  order  [Music]  and emotional accuracy  this is heart of darkness by Joseph  Conrad  so I fixed that so let me play you a few  and if you'd like you can look at some  of the mappings and try to assess out  parts of the novel but you're also just  welcome to us  [Music]  now on the other end of the spectrum and  then this next one is very different  because the protagonist is doing really  really horrible things in a happy tone  and I think it's captured in the music  [Music]  so I did a couple other projects using  this generating from text structure I  sauna fide the 2016 debates which was  more funny at the time  so overall Clinton was more positive and  active Trump was more negative and  passive but both candidates had high  levels of trust anticipation and fear  which I think makes sense when  discussing the future and I actually  didn't compare them to each other but I  compared them to themselves over the  course of the three debates I'm not  gonna play the whole thing each piece  for you I'll play a little bit just so  you can hear the difference  so the first debate this is Clinton's  first to be this is the baseline  so on the second debate her joy levels  rose  and then on the third debate her fear  levels rose for Trump Pierce's baseline  on the second debate his anger and fear  levels rose  and on the third debate his anger levels  rose above all other emotions  yeah let's move on from that so another  thing I did was a piece called sim  phonology with a French composer and  this was kind of the first time I'd  worked with a human composer and so  basically I created a couple of pieces  based on news articles talking about the  rise of technology over time and he  flesh those out into a full-scale  orchestra so this was a much longer  piece but I'll play you a little clip of  it  [Music]  [Music]  so after this I decided to do a couple  of pieces sorry a couple of pieces that  weren't text-based so this was one of  the first pieces that didn't use the  structure of text to generate music and  I intentionally chose this UFO sightings  data set to see if I could find  structure in a seemingly chaotic data  set and it turns out I can the pattern  in this data set is that most UFO  sightings happen in evening hours so it  creates this nice underlying rhythmic  structure these this is just a sample of  some of the sightings  so this is based on samples but I also  wanted to do a more formal composition  based on it so this is that piece  [Music]  so another thing I've been working on is  trying to generate music from film I'm  kind of creating automatic soundtracks  and this is a project that's still in  the early stages I'll play you just my  first prototype and this was my my  question to myself was basically is it  possible to just create a score using  the note C so I'll show you the results  of that can you turn that up a bit  [Music]  so this is all programmatically  generated based on the content of the  video  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Applause]  so it works better for some genres than  others so one piece I did this spring  that I loved was this piece called  fabrica alta and this is a factory one  of italy's like most well known  industrial factories that had kind of  been abandoned and more recently this  Italian collective d20 kind of revived  it and actually turned it into a musical  instrument and so they've been  commissioning like computer musicians to  generate pieces for it and what was cool  about this project was they they gave me  two sets of samples one of sounds of the  factory as it actually was when it was  actually in use you know like the looms  and the people speaking and footsteps  and things like that and then another  one of children pretending to be the  factory so I used a little bit of  machine learning with this I used just  like some basic clustering to kind of  see where the natural or the the older  and newer sounds combined and use that  to inform some of my decisions and I'll  play you a little bit of this piece so  this was the simulator they provided  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  and then this was actually played on the  building which was super cool  so I want to go back to the data for a  second so as I mentioned with transposed  I use this lexicon and I used it for  years without really examining it and  when I finally did I saw this and what  this is implying is that the word  childbirth along with several others was  tagged with no emotions which seemed  weird so this got me really interested  in the idea of subjective data and I'm  still exploring this space but a couple  of ideas and questions that have come up  in this research include you know how do  we incorporate emotions and other  subjective experiences into AI like  especially as AI starts moving into more  complex and personal spaces and  especially when we're modeling things  like personality and other people's life  experiences I'm really into this idea of  artisanal data which is kind of creating  and curating explicitly smaller datasets  to be creative for like artistic machine  learning and I'll show you one of those  in a bit deconstructing objectivity and  datasets in general like what type of  subjectivity do we find in really  commonly used machine learning data sets  how were they created what were the  motivations who ended up tagging these  things that are really informing our  society quite a bit at this point  historical bias I really firmly believe  that data sets create they basically  take a they capture a cultures values in  any certain time so how do we kind of  avoid that bias retention over time is  it possible to update datasets which  brings me to labels you know where did  this data come from what are the  ingredients in the data set what should  the expiration date of this data set be  this is something I'm really interested  in and exploring and of course who will  be allergic to this I think data set  should have labels like food has labels  and along the lines of labels I think  you know exploring creative taxonomies  is another way to explore subjective  datasets you know maybe instead of you  know asking for a gender or something  when you are getting demographics maybe  you ask for you know do you like the  ocean or mountain different more how  many years have you spent in a rural or  City environment you know trying to  change our the way we think about  demographics and things like that so  I've started to do some experiments in  this area and the first is this data set  of emotion tagged landscapes so there's  seven sets of landscapes fields forests  oceans roads lakes cities and mountains  and then eight emotions anger  anticipation joyfully fear disgust trust  surprise sadness and then also none and  so I created a couple thousand images  data set from Flickr and had people  tagged them with CrowdFlower  which is kind of like Mechanical Turk  except I like it slightly better because  it has a slightly tighter feedback loop  between the person who's creating the  job and the person who's tagging it and  the one real big obstacle in creating  this subjective data set was that it's  really hard to ask people to tag  emotions and this is made even harder  because CrowdFlower asks you to make  objective answers so it can weed out  bots and things like that and so I got a  lot of angry people you know you know  how can you say that this is incorrect  and so one thing I ended up doing was  saying that any answer is right as long  as they didn't select none and an  emotion and CrowdFlower also has this  nice like feedback loop where you are  rated by the Tigers so you can know that  you're not just like totally destroying  their lives so some of the results were  really cool like this is this is field  over a couple different emotions you see  the joy field trust field that to anger  fields at the bottom disgust had a lot  of Browns and  means a lot of discussed images were  either swamps or like dirt or water in  places it didn't really seem to belong  fear these are some fear results so we  have like a very obviously scary forest  at the top left here and then a pretty  scary like looming storm and then this  last one actually confused me for a  while until I realized that it's pretty  common to have a fear of just like the  wide open ocean so it was cool that the  Tigers were actually like getting both  visual emotions but also like conceptual  emotions as well and then surprise was  interesting because it had a lot of  bright colors so there were a lot of  things I learned about the data set and  I wanted to try to see if I could just  like transfer the style like could I  actually translate content between  emotions and so I did this using the  Nvidia fast I'll transfer that came out  maybe a year ago so on the top left is  or the top are the two inputs so that's  fear to Joy and the bottom is the output  so this is translating a forest from  fear to joy from joy to fear so the  bottom wants the output that's pretty  scary forest it didn't always work  especially with clouds this is a fear to  trust field and anger to joist city it's  just a little less gloomy joy to fear  forest sadness to joy ocean and then one  thing so the summer I was a scholar at  open AI and I focused on actually seeing  if I could generate landscapes kind of  from scratch like emotional landscapes  from scratch so my question to myself  was can i generate emotional variations  of the same base image and so I'm going  to show you a couple of these and so  each of the rows that I'm about to show  you represents the same set of base  generated images and they're pretty  small images  it because this is just a proof of  concept with a pretty small data set but  as these change if you keep your eye on  one or two of the columns you can see  how they change across emotions so this  is anger anticipation discussed a lot of  browns and greens again fear joy sadness  surprise and trust and I'll show you  quickly a second set I also tried to  generate specific landscapes so these  are generated mountains again angers  very dark anticipation with mountains  was actually not very bright discussed  again a lot of browns and greens sadness  is a little muted and then trust is kind  of calm and then I did the same thing  for generated forests so dark and blacks  and purples for anger lighter  anticipation browns and greens for  disgust darker for fear really bright  for joy a little muted for sadness  brighter colors for surprise and then  calmer for trust so that's it thank you  [Applause]
 

